course: dictionary
lesson: 5798
title: Words for people: madam
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Madam is a polite word for a woman. You use it when you do not know her name, such as when a shop worker or waiter speaks to a customer. The context shows this: «Can I help you, madam?» is what a shop assistant says to a customer.
explain: In a formal letter, a writer may begin with «Dear Sir or Madam» when they do not know who will read it. Here the word is still polite and shows respect.
explain: The same word can sound very different at home. A parent might say, «Don't you speak to me like that, young madam!» The clues are «don't» and «like that»: the child has been rude, so madam is a telling-off. Always ask what feeling the words carry, not just their dictionary meaning.
fact: Madam is a polite way to speak to a woman whose name you do not know.
fact: «Dear Sir or Madam» starts a formal letter when the writer does not know who will read it.
fact: Madam can come before a job title, as in Madam Chair, when you speak to a woman in an important job.
fact: In informal UK speech, «a little madam» is a girl who is bossy, cheeky or spoilt.
fact: Pani is the Polish word for madam, and pane means sir.
exercise: «Can I help you, madam?» Who is most likely saying this? | A teacher speaking to a pupil in class | A dog speaking to its owner | A pirate on a ship | *A shop assistant speaking to a customer
exercise: At home, a mum says, «Don't you speak to me like that, young madam!» What does young madam mean here? | A kind old friend | A grand lady at a party | A famous queen | *A bossy, cheeky girl
exercise: Which word in «Dear Sir or Madam» is used as a polite way to address a woman? | Or | Dear | *Madam | Sir
test: In «He tries his grand old manner, but the clerk laughs at the "madam"», what does the clerk's laugh suggest? | He is asleep | He is very proud of the word | *He finds the grand word funny, so he mocks it | He is crying
test: A child may say «pane» to the friend's grandad. What does pane mean? | *Sir | Grandad | Friend | Madam
exam: What is the best meaning of madam in «Dear Sir or Madam»? | *A polite way to address a woman whose name you do not know | A rude word for a shop worker | A bossy, cheeky girl | A woman in charge of a large house
